      "writtenOn": "2026-09-23T12:18:14Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e My understanding is that sanitize here is acting more as a fallback for invalid input than as part of the intended Type model. Its primary purpose seems to be preventing an already-accepted Type value from producing an invalid D-Bus object path segment.\n\n\nYeah in case of invalid input we can just bail out instead of trying to fix it. The earlier we bail out the better. If we accept arbitrary input and try to *correct* it by sanitizing we take 1 problem (the input) and make 2 problems out of it (the input, the sanitized input) and have to keep track of both, including their possible mismatch or equality and any further issues arising from that discrepancy.\n\nI\u0027m not asking you to rework the entire config parsing but it would be great if e.g. we could just check that these things equal their sanitized version instead of sanitizing and then handling 2 versions of the same value.\n\nFor example\n\n```\n\nif (!rawType.empty())\n{\n    if (dbus_util::sanitizeForDBusPathSegment(rawType) \u003d\u003d rawType) {\n        return rawType;\n    }\n}\n```",
